# Forms

Validating what a visitor typed, and saying what was wrong with it.

A form is the place a web application meets a stranger, so every value here arrives
untrusted and most of them arrive wrong. The job of this module is not to reject bad
input — the type system and the route signature already do that — but to collect
EVERY complaint about a submission and hand them back in a shape a page can render
beside the fields that caused them.

★ ERRORS ARE A SEQUENCE, NOT A MAP, AND THAT IS A SAFETY CHOICE. A LOGOS map TRAPS on
a missing key: `item "email" of errors` on a form that validated cleanly does not
answer `""`, it ends the program. A form template asks about a field precisely when
it does not know whether that field failed, so a map would make the ordinary case —
"render the error under this input, if any" — the crashing one. `errorFor` answers
`""` for a field with no complaint, which is the answer a template wants and can
concatenate without asking a second question first.

The sequence also keeps ORDER, so a summary at the top of a form lists complaints in
the order they were checked rather than in whatever order a hash chose.

`examples/signup_form` is the whole pattern in one page. A `## To validate` threads
one list through `checkRequired`, `checkEmail`, `checkMinLength` and `checkSame`,
starting from `noErrors()`; the submit action stores the answer in state; `errorFor`
writes each message under its input, a `{for each}` over the list is the summary,
and `isValid` of the same checks keeps the submit button disabled. The Forms section
of `docs/lgx-stdlib.md` builds it step by step.

## Note
One complaint about a submission: the `field` it concerns and the `message` to show beside it.

## A FieldError has:
    a field, which is Text.
    a message, which is Text.

## Note
An empty complaint list — the start of every validation.

## To noErrors () -> Seq of FieldError:
    Return a new Seq of FieldError.

## Note
Whether `errors` records no complaint at all.

## To isValid (errors: Seq of FieldError) -> Bool:
    Return length of errors is equal to 0.

## Note
Whether `field` has a complaint recorded against it.

## To hasError (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) -> Bool:
    For each e in errors:
        If e's field is equal to field:
            Return true.
    Return false.

## Note
The complaint against `field`, or `""` when there is none.

★ THE EMPTY ANSWER IS THE POINT. A template writes `{errorFor(errors, "email")}` under
the input and gets either the message or nothing, with no branch and no risk of asking
a collection for a key it does not hold. Returning the FIRST match matters too: one
field shows one message, because a visitor who is told three things about one box fixes
none of them.

## To errorFor (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) -> Text:
    For each e in errors:
        If e's field is equal to field:
            Return e's message.
    Return "".

## Note
`errors` with `message` recorded against `field`.

A field that already has a complaint is left alone, so the FIRST check to fail is the
one the visitor is shown. Order the checks from most fundamental to most particular —
`checkRequired` before `checkMinLength` — and the message will be the useful one rather
than "must be at least 8 characters" on an empty box.

## To addError (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (message: Text) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If hasError(errors, field) is equal to true:
        Return errors.
    Let mutable next be a new Seq of FieldError.
    For each e in errors:
        Push e to next.
    Push (a new FieldError with field field and message message) to next.
    Return next.

## Note
Whether `t` is empty or nothing but whitespace.

★ IT DELEGATES TO `strings.lg`'s `trim`. This module first grew its OWN trimmer and its own
`isSpaceChar` beside it — a second answer to a question the stdlib had already answered, which is
the exact shape this campaign keeps finding. It was also WRONG: the hand-written character
comparison never matched, so `trimmed("  hi  ")` answered `"  hi  "` and every check built on it
silently accepted whitespace as content. One trimmer, and it is the proven one.

## To isBlank (t: Text) -> Bool:
    Return length of trim(t) is equal to 0.

## Note
Complain when `value` is empty or all whitespace.

## To checkRequired (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (value: Text) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If isBlank(value) is equal to true:
        Return addError(errors, field, "This is required.")
    Return errors.

## Note
Complain when `value` is shorter than `n` characters, counting after trimming.

## To checkMinLength (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (value: Text) and (n: Int) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If length of trim(value) is less than n:
        Return addError(errors, field, "Use at least {n} characters.")
    Return errors.

## Note
Complain when `value` is longer than `n` characters, counting after trimming.

## To checkMaxLength (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (value: Text) and (n: Int) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If length of trim(value) is greater than n:
        Return addError(errors, field, "Use at most {n} characters.")
    Return errors.

## Note
Complain when `value` is outside `low` to `high`, both included.

## To checkRange (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (value: Int) and (low: Int) and (high: Int) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If value is less than low:
        Return addError(errors, field, "Choose a number from {low} to {high}.")
    If value is greater than high:
        Return addError(errors, field, "Choose a number from {low} to {high}.")
    Return errors.

## Note
Complain when `a` and `b` differ — the password-confirmation check.

## To checkSame (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (a: Text) and (b: Text) and (message: Text) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If a is not b:
        Return addError(errors, field, message)
    Return errors.

## Note
Whether `value`, trimmed, has the shape of an email address: one `@`, something before it, and a dot with text on both sides after it.

★ IT CHECKS THE SHAPE AND NOT THE ADDRESS, and says so rather than implying more. The
grammar of a real address is large enough that a validator matching it rejects valid
addresses far more often than it catches invalid ones, and the only proof that an
address works is a message arriving at it. So this asks the three questions worth
asking — one `@`, something before it, and a dotted something after it — and leaves the
rest to a confirmation email.

★ THE LOOP VARIABLE IS TEXT, NOT CHAR. `For each c in <Text>` yields a one-character TEXT,
and a Text compared with a Char is false for every character, with no error anywhere. So both
scans compare `c` with the Text `"@"` and `"."`.

## To looksLikeEmail (value: Text) -> Bool:
    Let t be trim(value).
    Let mutable pos be 0.
    Let mutable i be 0.
    Let mutable ats be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If c is equal to "@":
            Set pos to i.
            Set ats to ats + 1.
    If ats is not 1:
        Return false.
    If pos is at most 1:
        Return false.
    If pos is equal to length of t:
        Return false.
    Let mutable dot be false.
    Let mutable j be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set j to j + 1.
        If j is greater than pos:
            If c is equal to ".":
                If j is greater than pos + 1:
                    If j is less than length of t:
                        Set dot to true.
    Return dot.

## Note
Complain when `value` does not look like an email address. An empty value is left to
`checkRequired`, so an optional field may be blank without complaint.

## To checkEmail (errors: Seq of FieldError) and (field: Text) and (value: Text) -> Seq of FieldError:
    If isBlank(value) is equal to true:
        Return errors.
    If looksLikeEmail(value) is equal to false:
        Return addError(errors, field, "This does not look like an email address.")
    Return errors.

## Note
Every complaint, one per line — the summary a form puts above its fields.

## To errorSummary (errors: Seq of FieldError) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    For each e in errors:
        If length of out is greater than 0:
            Set out to out + "\n".
        Set out to out + e's message.
    Return out.
